WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.172
Most organizations already have integer-heavy legacy code.

2
00:00:03.172 --> 00:00:06.741
Improving it does not require stopping all feature work.

3
00:00:06.741 --> 00:00:07.931
It requires prioritization.

4
00:00:07.931 --> 00:00:15.466
Start with paths where integers influence memory allocation, buffer bounds, parser state, loop limits, permissions, quota decisions, resource limits,

5
00:00:15.466 --> 00:00:17.845
persistence formats, and cross-platform behavior.

6
00:00:17.845 --> 00:00:21.414
During review, look for values whose domain is unclear.

7
00:00:21.414 --> 00:00:27.759
A plain int might represent an index, byte count, status code, timeout, offset, or business identifier.

8
00:00:27.759 --> 00:00:35.293
Replacing unclear types with domain-appropriate types, clearer aliases, wrapper objects, or named constants can reduce guesswork for future

9
00:00:35.293 --> 00:00:35.690
maintainers.

10
00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:38.466
Add range checks before behavior-sensitive conversions.

11
00:00:38.466 --> 00:00:47.190
If a value is parsed from outside the process, loaded from storage, received from another component, or derived from user-controlled configuration,

12
00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.966
the conversion point should not be casual.

13
00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:55.121
The code should show why the destination type can safely represent the value.

14
00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:58.690
Refactoring should be incremental and protected by regression tests.

15
00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:03.845
For a risky size calculation, add tests around current behavior before changing it.

16
00:01:03.845 --> 00:01:09.793
Then wrap the calculation, add checked arithmetic, clarify units, and verify that callers handle failure.

17
00:01:09.793 --> 00:01:13.759
Small changes are easier to review and safer to deploy.

18
00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:16.138
Some assumptions cannot be removed immediately.

19
00:01:16.138 --> 00:01:26.448
When that happens, document the assumption near the code, link it to a follow-up item if appropriate, and add tests or diagnostics that make future

20
00:01:26.448 --> 00:01:27.241
changes safer.

21
00:01:27.241 --> 00:01:32.000
Legacy improvement is a steady engineering practice, not a one-time cleanup.
